<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- branch reaper (#1787): a daily LOCAL-branch pass over the tortoise repo.
         The reaper itself is agent-infra/scripts/cleanup-stale-branches.sh,
         farmed to {{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/ by pi-bootstrap/setup.sh's
         fleet-scripts farm.

         WHY LOCAL SCOPE. This estate squash-merges, so a merged branch is NOT
         an ancestor of main and the remote scope's `merged` criterion is
         nearly blind to it (measured 2026-10-15: tortoise carries 1,153
         branches with a MERGED PR while that criterion reports 199). The local
         scope selects a stale branch only when a MERGED pull request exists
         for it, or it has no commits outside origin/main, or its upstream
         already holds everything it does — and it never selects a branch
         checked out in a worktree, main, a branch with an OPEN PR, or one
         whose local commits exist nowhere else.

         WHY THE ENTRY POINT IS THE CHECKOUT'S OWN PYTHON, NOT `bash`.
         macOS TCC is per-binary and denies a launchd-spawned `bash` the read of
         a script under ~/Documents ("Operation not permitted") AND a
         launchd-spawned `git` the read of the repo there ("not a git
         repository"). The reaper's whole job is `git` against that checkout, so
         a `bash <script>` entry point cannot work no matter where the script
         lives. The binary that DOES hold the access is the checkout's own
         interpreter ({{TORTOISE_REPO}}/.venv/bin/python), the same one the
         working com.tortoise.worktree-reaper agent runs. So launchd starts that
         interpreter on pi-reap-worktrees-launchd.py, which reads the script
         itself and hands it to bash on stdin; bash and the `git` it spawns
         inherit the access from that process.

         WHY `repo` IS PASSED. The shared launcher runs the script with
         `bash -s`, and under `bash -s` BASH_SOURCE is EMPTY, so the script's own
         checkout inference has nothing to resolve. The repo argument is the seam that
         tells it which checkout to sweep.

         SHIPS IN DRY-RUN. The argument list below carries the scope flag but NO
         execute flag, so the pass classifies and reports and deletes nothing.
         Arming it is a deliberate, per-machine operator decision: add the
         reaper's execute flag HERE and re-run scripts/install-launchd.sh.
         Removing nothing is NOT arming — with no execute flag the reaper
         dry-runs, which is the shipped state.

         The repo argument is {{TORTOISE_REPO}}. A machine without a tortoise
         checkout leaves that placeholder unresolved, and install-launchd.sh
         then SKIPS this template by design (unresolved placeholder) instead of
         installing a broken target.

         WHERE THE REPORT GOES. The reaper PRINTS its report; it writes no file
         unless CLEANUP_BRANCH_LOG is set. So this job sets it: a scheduled pass
         appends its report to
         {{HOME}}/.pi/agent/state/cleanup-stale-branches.log, and the script
         TRUNCATES that file before a pass once it exceeds
         CLEANUP_BRANCH_LOG_MAX_BYTES (1 MiB default), so it holds the most
         recent passes and cannot grow without bound — the same shape as the
         worktree reaper's REAP_WT_LOG. No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath for
         the same reason the other reapers omit them: an unbounded
         launchd-captured log is the surface they already avoid, and the script's
         own file is the retrievable, bounded copy. Before this key existed an
         unarmed pass printed to the unified log only, which nobody can retrieve. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.tortoise.branch-reaper</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>{{TORTOISE_REPO}}/.venv/bin/python</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/pi-reap-worktrees-launchd.py</string>
        <string>--script</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/cleanup-stale-branches.sh</string>
        <string>--repo</string>
        <string>{{TORTOISE_REPO}}</string>
        <string>--scope</string>
        <string>local</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- The reaper shells out to `gh`, `jq` and `git`; a launchd job gets
             neither the operator's PATH nor their rc files, so PATH is rendered
             from the installer's own (with the unreadable pi shim dir removed
             by install-launchd.sh, #1719/#427). -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <!-- The retrievable, capped report log (see the header note). The script
             creates the state directory, so the file always lands. -->
        <key>CLEANUP_BRANCH_LOG</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/state/cleanup-stale-branches.log</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <!-- 86400s = daily. Branch accumulation is slow (the 2026-10-15 sweep found
         2,125 in tortoise after a long gap), so a daily classification pass is
         enough to keep ~/.pi/agent/state/cleanup-stale-branches.log (the report
         this job writes) current without a busy job. -->
    <integer>86400</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
